      <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
      <section class="content-section" id="introduction">

        <h2>1. Introduction to Placement Preparation</h2>

        <p>
          Placement preparation is the process of developing the
          knowledge, technical skills and communication skills required
          to obtain a job after completing college.
        </p>

        <p>
          Students generally prepare for aptitude tests, coding rounds,
          technical interviews, communication rounds and HR interviews.
          A good preparation strategy helps students perform confidently
          throughout the recruitment process.
        </p>

        <div class="info-box">
          <strong>Tip:</strong>
          Start placement preparation early instead of waiting until
          the final semester.
        </div>

        <h3>Important Areas</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Aptitude and quantitative ability</li>
          <li>Logical reasoning</li>
          <li>Verbal ability</li>
          <li>Programming and coding</li>
          <li>Data structures and algorithms</li>
          <li>Database management systems</li>
          <li>Operating systems</li>
          <li>Computer networks</li>
          <li>Communication skills</li>
          <li>Resume preparation</li>
          <li>Technical interviews</li>
          <li>HR interviews</li>
        </ul>

      </section>


      <!-- PLACEMENT PROCESS -->
      <section class="content-section" id="placement-process">

        <h2>2. Placement Process</h2>

        <p>
          Most campus recruitment processes contain several rounds.
          The exact process depends on the company and job role.
        </p>

        <table>

          <tr>
            <th>Round</th>
            <th>What is Tested?</th>
          </tr>

          <tr>
            <td>Aptitude Test</td>
            <td>Mathematics, reasoning and verbal ability</td>
          </tr>

          <tr>
            <td>Coding Test</td>
            <td>Programming and problem-solving</td>
          </tr>

          <tr>
            <td>Technical Interview</td>
            <td>Computer science and technical knowledge</td>
          </tr>

          <tr>
            <td>Communication Round</td>
            <td>English and communication skills</td>
          </tr>

          <tr>
            <td>HR Interview</td>
            <td>Personality, attitude and career interests</td>
          </tr>

        </table>

        <h3>Typical Process</h3>

        <div class="code-box">Aptitude Test
       ↓
Coding Test
       ↓
Technical Interview
       ↓
HR Interview
       ↓
Job Offer</div>

      </section>


      <!-- APTITUDE -->
      <section class="content-section" id="aptitude">

        <h2>3. Aptitude Preparation</h2>

        <p>
          Aptitude tests are commonly used during the initial stage of
          recruitment. They check mathematical ability and problem-solving
          skills.
        </p>

        <h3>Important Aptitude Topics</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Percentages</li>
          <li>Profit and Loss</li>
          <li>Simple Interest</li>
          <li>Compound Interest</li>
          <li>Time and Work</li>
          <li>Time, Speed and Distance</li>
          <li>Ratio and Proportion</li>
          <li>Averages</li>
          <li>Number Systems</li>
          <li>Probability</li>
          <li>Permutations and Combinations</li>
          <li>Data Interpretation</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Example</h3>

        <p>
          If a product costs ₹500 and its price increases by 10%, the
          new price is:
        </p>

        <div class="code-box">Increase = 10% of 500
        = (10 / 100) × 500
        = 50

New Price = 500 + 50
         = ₹550</div>

        <div class="info-box">
          <strong>Practice Tip:</strong>
          Solve problems regularly and use a timer to improve speed.
        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- REASONING -->
      <section class="content-section" id="reasoning">

        <h2>4. Logical Reasoning</h2>

        <p>
          Logical reasoning tests the ability to analyze information,
          identify patterns and solve problems logically.
        </p>

        <h3>Important Topics</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Number series</li>
          <li>Alphabet series</li>
          <li>Coding and decoding</li>
          <li>Blood relations</li>
          <li>Directions</li>
          <li>Seating arrangement</li>
          <li>Logical puzzles</li>
          <li>Syllogisms</li>
          <li>Odd one out</li>
          <li>Data sufficiency</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Preparation Strategy</h3>

        <ol>
          <li>Understand the question carefully.</li>
          <li>Identify the given information.</li>
          <li>Look for patterns.</li>
          <li>Eliminate incorrect options.</li>
          <li>Practice different types of problems.</li>
        </ol>

      </section>


      <!-- VERBAL -->
      <section class="content-section" id="verbal">

        <h2>5. Verbal Ability</h2>

        <p>
          Verbal ability measures a candidate's understanding and use
          of the English language.
        </p>

        <h3>Important Topics</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Grammar</li>
          <li>Vocabulary</li>
          <li>Synonyms and antonyms</li>
          <li>Sentence correction</li>
          <li>Reading comprehension</li>
          <li>Fill in the blanks</li>
          <li>Para jumbles</li>
          <li>Sentence completion</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>How to Improve English</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Read English articles regularly.</li>
          <li>Learn a few new words every day.</li>
          <li>Practice speaking in English.</li>
          <li>Watch English educational videos.</li>
          <li>Practice grammar questions.</li>
        </ul>

      </section>


      <!-- CODING -->
      <section class="content-section" id="coding">

        <h2>6. Coding Preparation</h2>

        <p>
          Coding tests are used by many companies to evaluate
          programming knowledge and problem-solving ability.
        </p>

        <h3>Recommended Programming Topics</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Variables and data types</li>
          <li>Operators</li>
          <li>Conditional statements</li>
          <li>Loops</li>
          <li>Arrays</li>
          <li>Strings</li>
          <li>Functions and methods</li>
          <li>Object-oriented programming</li>
          <li>Recursion</li>
          <li>Data structures</li>
          <li>Algorithms</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Simple Java Example</h3>

        <div class="code-box">class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int a = 10;
        int b = 20;

        int sum = a + b;

        System.out.println("Sum = " + sum);
    }
}</div>

        <h3>Important Coding Problems</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Reverse a number</li>
          <li>Check palindrome</li>
          <li>Check prime number</li>
          <li>Find factorial</li>
          <li>Fibonacci series</li>
          <li>Find largest element in an array</li>
          <li>Sort an array</li>
          <li>Count vowels in a string</li>
          <li>Reverse a string</li>
          <li>Remove duplicate elements</li>
        </ul>

      </section>


      <!-- TECHNICAL -->
      <section class="content-section" id="technical">

        <h2>7. Technical Interview Preparation</h2>

        <p>
          A technical interview checks whether the candidate understands
          the fundamentals related to their degree and job role.
        </p>

        <h3>Important Computer Science Subjects</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Programming languages</li>
          <li>Object-Oriented Programming</li>
          <li>Data Structures</li>
          <li>Algorithms</li>
          <li>DBMS</li>
          <li>SQL</li>
          <li>Operating Systems</li>
          <li>Computer Networks</li>
          <li>Software Engineering</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Common Technical Questions</h3>

        <ol>
          <li>What is Object-Oriented Programming?</li>
          <li>What is inheritance?</li>
          <li>What is polymorphism?</li>
          <li>What is an interface in Java?</li>
          <li>What is a database?</li>
          <li>What is normalization?</li>
          <li>What is a primary key?</li>
          <li>What is a data structure?</li>
          <li>What is an array?</li>
          <li>What is the difference between stack and queue?</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="info-box">
          <strong>Important:</strong>
          Do not memorize only definitions. Try to understand concepts
          and practice explaining them with simple examples.
        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- COMMUNICATION -->
      <section class="content-section" id="communication">

        <h2>8. Communication Skills</h2>

        <p>
          Good communication helps candidates express their knowledge
          clearly during interviews and group discussions.
        </p>

        <h3>Ways to Improve Communication</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Speak English regularly.</li>
          <li>Practice self-introduction.</li>
          <li>Participate in group discussions.</li>
          <li>Read articles and news.</li>
          <li>Practice interview questions.</li>
          <li>Record yourself while speaking.</li>
          <li>Work on pronunciation and vocabulary.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Simple Self-Introduction Structure</h3>

        <div class="code-box">1. Greeting
2. Name
3. Education
4. Technical skills
5. Projects
6. Hobbies
7. Career interest
8. Thank you</div>

      </section>


      <!-- RESUME -->
      <section class="content-section" id="resume">

        <h2>9. Resume Preparation</h2>

        <p>
          A resume gives recruiters a quick overview of a candidate's
          education, skills, projects and achievements.
        </p>

        <h3>Important Resume Sections</h3>

        <ol>
          <li>Name and contact information</li>
          <li>Career objective or summary</li>
          <li>Education</li>
          <li>Technical skills</li>
          <li>Projects</li>
          <li>Internships</li>
          <li>Certifications</li>
          <li>Achievements</li>
          <li>Extra-curricular activities</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Resume Tips</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Keep the resume clear and easy to read.</li>
          <li>Use accurate information.</li>
          <li>Highlight relevant technical skills.</li>
          <li>Describe projects clearly.</li>
          <li>Check spelling and grammar.</li>
          <li>Avoid unnecessary information.</li>
          <li>Keep formatting consistent.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="info-box">
          <strong>Tip:</strong>
          Create a resume that matches the job role you are applying for.
        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- HR INTERVIEW -->
      <section class="content-section" id="hr-interview">

        <h2>10. HR Interview Preparation</h2>

        <p>
          The HR interview focuses on communication, personality,
          attitude, career interests and how well a candidate fits
          the organization.
        </p>

        <h3>Common HR Questions</h3>

        <ol>
          <li>Tell me about yourself.</li>
          <li>What are your strengths?</li>
          <li>What are your weaknesses?</li>
          <li>Why should we hire you?</li>
          <li>Why do you want to join our company?</li>
          <li>Where do you see yourself in the future?</li>
          <li>Tell me about your project.</li>
          <li>Why did you choose Computer Science?</li>
          <li>Are you comfortable working in a team?</li>
          <li>Do you have any questions for us?</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>How to Answer</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Speak clearly and confidently.</li>
          <li>Give honest answers.</li>
          <li>Keep answers relevant.</li>
          <li>Use examples when possible.</li>
          <li>Maintain professional body language.</li>
        </ul>

      </section>


      <!-- MOCK INTERVIEW -->
      <section class="content-section" id="mock-interview">

        <h2>11. Mock Interview</h2>

        <p>
          A mock interview is a practice interview that helps students
          become comfortable with the actual recruitment process.
        </p>

        <h3>Benefits</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Improves confidence.</li>
          <li>Reduces interview anxiety.</li>
          <li>Improves communication.</li>
          <li>Helps identify weak areas.</li>
          <li>Improves answer structure.</li>
          <li>Provides practice with common questions.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Practice Method</h3>

        <ol>
          <li>Select common interview questions.</li>
          <li>Prepare short and clear answers.</li>
          <li>Practice speaking aloud.</li>
          <li>Record your answers.</li>
          <li>Review your performance.</li>
          <li>Improve weak areas.</li>
        </ol>

      </section>


      <!-- JOB PREPARATION -->
      <section class="content-section" id="job-preparation">

        <h2>12. Job Preparation</h2>

                <p>
          Job preparation involves understanding the job role, researching
          the company and developing the skills required for the position.
          Students should carefully read the job description and prepare
          according to the requirements of the company.
        </p>

        <h3>Before Applying for a Job</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Read the job description carefully.</li>
          <li>Check the required technical skills.</li>
          <li>Prepare a suitable resume.</li>
          <li>Learn about the company.</li>
          <li>Practice relevant coding problems.</li>
          <li>Revise important technical subjects.</li>
          <li>Improve communication skills.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Company Research</h3>

        <p>
          Before attending an interview, students should learn about the
          company's products, services, work culture and the responsibilities
          of the job role. This preparation helps candidates answer interview
          questions more confidently.
        </p>

        <h3>Important Skills for Software Jobs</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Programming fundamentals</li>
          <li>Data structures and algorithms</li>
          <li>Database and SQL knowledge</li>
          <li>Object-oriented programming</li>
          <li>Problem-solving skills</li>
          <li>Communication skills</li>
          <li>Teamwork</li>
          <li>Time management</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="info-box">
          <strong>Tip:</strong>
          Apply for jobs that match your skills and carefully follow the
          application instructions provided by the company.
        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- COMMON MISTAKES -->
      <section class="content-section" id="common-mistakes">

        <h2>13. Common Placement Mistakes</h2>

        <p>
          Avoiding common mistakes can improve your performance during
          placement tests and interviews.
        </p>

        <h3>Common Mistakes</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Starting placement preparation too late.</li>
          <li>Ignoring communication skills.</li>
          <li>Not practicing coding regularly.</li>
          <li>Memorizing concepts without understanding them.</li>
          <li>Using an unorganized resume.</li>
          <li>Not researching the company before an interview.</li>
          <li>Giving very long answers during interviews.</li>
          <li>Not practicing mock interviews.</li>
          <li>Providing incorrect information on the resume.</li>
          <li>Not managing time properly during tests.</li>
        </ul>

      </section>


      <!-- DAILY STUDY PLAN -->
      <section class="content-section">

        <h2>Daily Placement Preparation Plan</h2>

        <p>
          A simple daily routine can help students prepare consistently
          for placement examinations and interviews.
        </p>

        <table>

          <tr>
            <th>Activity</th>
            <th>Suggested Time</th>
          </tr>

          <tr>
            <td>Aptitude</td>
            <td>30 minutes</td>
          </tr>

          <tr>
            <td>Logical Reasoning</td>
